Z||000004||87ECO5||05-22-2001||12:50 PM||||Sounds like an interesting idea, Chuck.  I'll offer a 150 mile radius around Cincinnati, Ohio:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87ECO5_region.jpg"> <P>I only see two problems.<P>#1 - I don't have a truck.  I have a mid-sized SUV that will hold a few "medium-sized" items (no rear clips, hoods, etc. though).  <P>#2 - with an MPG rating of 22, it will be somewhat expensive (a little over $12 just to cover half of my region using $1.80 gasoline).<P>I'm willing to try just about anything.  Heck, if nothing else, it's an excuse just to get out of butt-ugly Dayton, Ohio for an afternoon!||||reg||14
Z||000005||Raydar||05-22-2001||12:58 PM||||Sure! Sounds like a great idea! I live in Douglasville GA. About halfway between Atlanta and the Alabama line. Would be willing to drive an hour or so to shuffle parts. Maybe even more, if there's not a big time constraint. I work in downtown Atlanta, and drive an S-10 truck, so if it would be convenient for someone to drive downtown to meet me during the weekdays, I could move some parts -even big parts like body clips. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cheever2||05-22-2001||01:06 PM||||In the ideal situation, you would not go to the trouble to just carry one part for one person, but rather do it for several items and for several people on the same trip.  After all, the Pony Express riders didn't carry just one letter.  <P>And if enough relay participants are involved, hopefully you wouldn't often be asked to travel all the way to the edge of that radius, just to where someone else can take over.<P>Some of us would REQUIRE reimbursement for our costs, but hopefully some can volunteer.  That would be between them and the requestor of the parts, and would thus be confidential.<P>I realize there are size and weight limitations.  That is why the more are involved, the better the chances it will succeed.  Maybe someone else in your area has a trailer or pickup for those big items, and you won't be burdened every relay request.<P>So far I get the impression this could work.  More opinions?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||JSocha||05-22-2001||03:01 PM||||I don't know.<P>I think you better count me out.  I've got a problem regarding location where I could be the epicenter of parts delivery or routing.<P>I'm in the Geographical Center of North America (Rugby).<P>What does that mean you ask?<P>Our city is equally distance from all costal points (1,500 miles) in all directions and was determined by a geological survey where they determined our/this location by balancing a map of North America on a pin.  Rugby just happened to fall as the balancing point under this map and now we have the "Marking Monument".  <P>Drew Carry recently seen it when he drove through our little city a few weeks ago in his Porche while on vacation.<P>Perhaps, we should find "Celebrity's" to take our parts while there on vacation!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||9
Z||000008||Raydar||05-22-2001||03:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<BR><B>I'm in the Geographical Center of North America (Rugby).<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Perfect!!! You can carry them ALL!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||14
